{
  "id": "modalverben",
  "name": "pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, savoir + infinitive; il faut",
  "level": "A2",
  "variety": "fr-FR",
  "subtitle": "To say what you can, want, must or know how to do, use a conjugated verb + an infinitive: Je peux venir lundi. Elle veut changer de médecin. Nous devons partir. The second verb never changes. Il faut + infinitive says what has to be done.",
  "briefMarks": ["je peux", "je veux", "je dois", "je sais", "il faut", "pouvoir", "vouloir", "devoir", "savoir"],
  "source": "icanspeak French original text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-11-08)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [{"text": "subject", "variant": "dat"},{"text": "peux · veux · dois · sais", "variant": "verb"},{"text": "infinitive", "variant": "prep"}],
    "exampleHtml": "Je <span class=\"vp-verb\">peux</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">venir</span> lundi. · Elle <span class=\"vp-verb\">veut</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">changer</span> de médecin. · Nous <span class=\"vp-verb\">devons</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">apporter</span> nos papiers. · Il <span class=\"vp-verb\">sait</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">nager</span>. · Il <span class=\"vp-verb\">faut</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">prendre</span> rendez-vous.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, savoir</strong> change with the subject. The verb after them stays in the <strong>infinitive</strong>, with no <em>à</em> or <em>de</em>: <em>Je peux venir. Tu dois attendre. Ils veulent partir.</em>",
        "tag": "Verb + infinitive"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>pouvoir</strong> = can, may (it is possible or allowed): <em>Je peux venir lundi. Vous pouvez entrer.</em> <strong>vouloir</strong> = want: <em>Je veux changer de médecin.</em> <strong>devoir</strong> = must, have to: <em>Je dois apporter mes papiers.</em>",
        "tag": "Meanings"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>savoir</strong> = know how to, a skill you have learnt. <strong>pouvoir</strong> = it is possible now. <em>Je sais nager</em> (I have learnt) / <em>Je peux venir</em> (I am free). <em>Il sait conduire</em> / <em>Il ne peut pas conduire ce soir.</em>",
        "tag": "savoir or pouvoir?"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>il faut</strong> + infinitive says what has to be done, for anybody. It never changes: <em>Il faut remplir ce formulaire. Il faut apporter un justificatif.</em> <em>Il ne faut pas</em> means <em>you must not</em>: <em>Il ne faut pas fumer ici.</em>",
        "tag": "il faut"
      },
      {
        "html": "In the negative, <strong>ne … pas</strong> goes around the conjugated verb. The infinitive comes after: <em>Je ne peux pas venir. Il ne veut pas attendre.</em> See <a href=\"/ne-pas\">negation</a>.",
        "tag": "Negative"
      },
      {
        "html": "For polite requests, <strong>je voudrais</strong> + noun or infinitive is softer than <em>je veux</em>: <em>Je voudrais un rendez-vous. Je voudrais parler à la directrice.</em> More in <a href=\"/polite-requests\">polite requests</a>.",
        "tag": "je voudrais"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Je peux de venir</em> → ✓ <em>Je peux venir</em>. ✗ <em>Il faut à prendre rendez-vous</em> → ✓ <em>Il faut prendre rendez-vous</em>. ✗ <em>Je sais venir lundi</em> → ✓ <em>Je peux venir lundi</em>. ✗ <em>Je peut</em> → ✓ <em>Je peux</em>. ✗ <em>Tu veut</em> → ✓ <em>Tu veux</em>. In fast speech people say <em>Je peux pas</em>; in writing and at the préfecture say <em>Je ne peux pas</em>. Next: <a href=\"/polite-requests\">polite requests</a>, <a href=\"/ne-pas\">negation</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
